{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 48 kg, height 163 cm (1.63 m). BMI = 48 kg ÷ (1.63 m)² ≈ 48 ÷ 2.6569 ≈ 18.1 kg/m². This is <18.5, so the patient is underweight. 2. For underweight patients, the adjusted body weight = actual body weight = 48 kg. 3. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for men: CrCl (mL/min) = [(140 – age) × weight (kg)] ÷ (72 × serum creatinine (mg/dL)). 4. Plug in the values: age = 87 years, weight = 48 kg, serum creatinine = 1.4 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 87) × 48 = 53 × 48 = 2544. Denominator = 72 × 1.4 = 100.8. 5. CrCl = 2544 ÷ 100.8 ≈ 25.2 mL/min. 6. Rounded to the nearest whole number, the creatinine clearance is about 25 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈ 25 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 105 kg / (1.60 m)^2 = 41 kg/m² → obese. 2. Use adjusted body weight (ABW) because BMI >30. 3. Ideal body weight (IBW) for women = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height = 5 ft 3 in (63 in). IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg×3 = 52.4 kg. 4. ABW = IBW + 0.4 × (actual weight – IBW) = 52.4 kg + 0.4 × (105 kg – 52.4 kg) = 52.4 kg + 0.4 × 52.6 kg = 52.4 kg + 21.04 kg = 73.44 kg. 5. Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = ((140 – age) × weight × 0.85) / (72 × serum creatinine). 6. Plug in values: age = 42, weight = 73.44 kg, serum creatinine = 0.8 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 42) × 73.44 × 0.85 = 98 × 73.44 × 0.85 ≈ 6,117.55. Denominator = 72 × 0.8 = 57.6. 7. CrCl = 6,117.55 / 57.6 ≈ 106.2 mL/min. 8. Round to nearest whole number: ≈106 mL/min.",
  "answer": "106 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Extract the required data at admission: age = 71 years, sex = male, serum creatinine = 1.42 mg/dL, height = 155 cm, weight = 59 kg. 2. Calculate BMI = weight (kg) / (height (m))² = 59 kg / (1.55 m)² ≈ 24.5 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5–24.9). 3. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight (ABW) to the minimum of ideal body weight (IBW) and actual weight. 4. Compute IBW for a man: IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height in inches = 155 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 61.0 in. IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (61.0 – 60) ≈ 52.35 kg. 5. Minimum of IBW (52.35 kg) and actual weight (59 kg) = 52.35 kg, so ABW = 52.35 kg. 6. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for males: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW] ÷ (72 × serum creatinine). Numerator = (140 – 71) × 52.35 = 69 × 52.35 ≈ 3612.15. Denominator = 72 × 1.42 = 102.24. 7. CrCl = 3612.15 ÷ 102.24 ≈ 35.3 mL/min. 8. Round to a reasonable clinical precision: about 35 mL/min.",
  "answer": "35.3 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Determine the patient's BMI: weight 53 kg / (1.53 m)^2 = 22.6 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 2. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight (ABW) to the minimum of the ideal body weight (IBW) and the actual weight. 3. Calculate IBW for a woman: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height = 153 cm = 60.24 in, so IBW ≈ 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × 0.24 ≈ 46.0 kg. 4. Minimum of IBW (46.0 kg) and actual weight (53 kg) = 46.0 kg. Thus ABW = 46.0 kg. 5. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW × 0.85] / (SCr × 72). 6. Plug in values: age = 82 yr, ABW = 46.0 kg, serum creatinine (SCr) = 3.26 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 82) × 46.0 × 0.85 = 58 × 46.0 × 0.85 = 2,267.8. Denominator = 3.26 × 72 = 234.72. 7. CrCl = 2,267.8 / 234.72 ≈ 9.66 mL/min. 8. Round to a reasonable clinical precision: ≈ 9.7 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈ 9.7 mL/min"
}

